---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ias/ias27.json";

---

<StandardPageLayout {...metadata}>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        {
          id: "s1",
          label: "Conceptual Framework",
        },
        {
          id: "s2",
          label: "Scope & Definitions",
        },
        {
          id: "s3",
          label: "When Separate Statements Are Prepared",
        },
        {
          id: "s4",
          label: "Measurement of Investments",
        },
        {
          id: "s5",
          label: "Dividend Recognition",
        },
        {
          id: "s6",
          label: "Group Reorganisations",
        },
        {
          id: "s7",
          label: "Changes in Measurement Method",
        },
        {
          id: "s8",
          label: "Separate vs Consolidated vs Individual",
        },
        {
          id: "s9",
          label: "Disclosure",
        },
        {
          id: "s10",
          label: "Judgments & Estimates",
        },
        {
          id: "s11",
          label: "IFRS vs US GAAP",
        },
        {
          id: "s12",
          label: "Statement Mapping",
        },
        {
          id: "s13",
          label: "Related Standards",
        },
        {
          id: "s14",
          label: "Glossary",
        },
        {
          id: "history",
          label: "History & Development",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="wrap">
    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why a legal entity might report differently from its group</div>
        <div class="principle">The Conceptual Framework's REPORTING ENTITY concept recognises that consolidated statements represent the ECONOMIC group as a single unit, while a parent's own SEPARATE financial statements represent the LEGAL entity in isolation. IAS 27 exists to prescribe accounting for investments in subsidiaries, joint ventures, and associates when a parent chooses (or is required by local law) to present both.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IAS 27 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:600">Reporting entity boundary</strong> — separate financial statements deliberately do NOT consolidate; they report the parent's investment as a single-line item, reflecting the LEGAL entity's own resources and claims</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:600">Comparability</strong> — offering only three permitted measurement options (Section 4) preserves comparability across entities that choose to prepare separate statements</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:600">Faithful representation</strong> — separate financial statements are explicitly NOT a substitute for consolidated statements when consolidation is otherwise required</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">A very common point of confusion: "separate" financial statements are NOT the same as "individual" or "unconsolidated" financial statements of an entity with NO subsidiaries — IAS 27 specifically addresses financial statements presented IN ADDITION TO consolidated statements, by an entity WITH investments in subsidiaries/associates/joint ventures.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Measurement principles applied</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 27 restricts measurement of qualifying investments to a short, closed list of options — deliberately narrower than the general measurement flexibility seen elsewhere in IFRS.</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Framework basis</span><span>Used by IAS 27 for…</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Historical cost</span><span>Investments in subsidiaries, joint ventures, and associates — Option 1</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Fair value (IFRS 9)</span><span>Same investments — Option 2</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Equity method (IAS 28 mechanics)</span><span>Same investments — Option 3, added by a 2014 amendment</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info" style="margin-bottom:0;"> <strong style="font-weight:600">Qualitative characteristics in action:</strong> restricting the choice to just three options (rather than an open-ended policy choice) balances <strong style="font-weight:600">relevance</strong> for preparers who genuinely have different information needs, against <strong style="font-weight:600">comparability</strong> for users comparing separate financial statements across entities.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope & Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IAS 27 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 27 prescribes the accounting and disclosure requirements for investments in subsidiaries, joint ventures, and associates when an entity PREPARES separate financial statements — it does not mandate WHO must prepare them; that is determined by local regulation or a voluntary election.</div>
        <div class="elabel">WHAT IAS 27 DOES NOT COVER</div>
        <div class="cross-list">
          <span>How to prepare CONSOLIDATED financial statements → <strong style="font-weight:600">IFRS 10</strong></span>
          <span>Accounting for investments in associates and joint ventures using the equity method WITHIN consolidated statements → <strong style="font-weight:600">IAS 28</strong></span>
          <span>Determining whether an investee is a subsidiary, associate, or joint venture in the first place → <strong style="font-weight:600">IFRS 10 / IFRS 11 / IAS 28</strong></span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IAS 27 is genuinely narrow in scope — it is the ONLY question of "how does the PARENT (the single legal entity) measure its investment in the group companies in its OWN standalone accounts?"</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Separate financial statements</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">Those presented by a parent (an investor with control of a subsidiary), or an investor with joint control of, or significant influence over, an investee, in which the investments are accounted for at cost, in accordance with IFRS 9, or using the equity method.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Consolidated financial statements</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The financial statements of a group in which the assets, liabilities, equity, income, expenses, and cash flows of the parent and its subsidiaries are presented as those of a single economic entity — governed by IFRS 10, not IAS 27.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Cost method</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">Historical cost, less accumulated impairment losses — investment income is recognised only to the extent the investor receives distributions from ACCUMULATED PROFITS of the investee arising AFTER the acquisition date.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>When Separate Financial Statements Are Prepared</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Voluntary or mandated by local law — never a substitute for consolidation</div>
        <div class="principle">Separate financial statements are prepared IN ADDITION TO consolidated financial statements (when consolidation is required), or in addition to financial statements in which investments in associates/joint ventures are accounted for using the equity method under IAS 28 — separate financial statements are NEVER a substitute for these when they are otherwise required.</div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-bottom:0;"> <strong style="font-weight:600">A parent with no subsidiaries, associates, or joint ventures does NOT prepare "separate financial statements"</strong> in the IAS 27 sense — its ordinary financial statements ARE simply its financial statements, not a special "separate" category, since there is nothing to consolidate or equity-account in the first place.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Measurement of Investments — The Three Permitted Options</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The three options</div>
        <div class="dot-list" style="margin-bottom:9px;">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:600">At cost</strong> — historical cost, less any accumulated impairment</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:600">In accordance with IFRS 9</strong> — fair value through profit or loss, or (for equity investments) an irrevocable FVOCI election</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:600">Using the equity method</strong> as described in IAS 28 — a 2014 amendment reintroduced this as a permitted option</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-bottom:0;"> The SAME method must be applied for EACH CATEGORY of investment (subsidiaries, joint ventures, associates), though DIFFERENT methods MAY be used for different categories — a genuine, deliberate flexibility within the standard.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Worked example — cost method with dividend</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Investment at cost, dividend received</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            A parent acquires 100% of a subsidiary for $5,000,000 cash, recording the investment at cost in its separate financial statements. In the following year, the subsidiary declares and pays a dividend of $300,000 to the parent, out of profits earned AFTER the acquisition date.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Investment carrying amount</strong> = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">$5,000,000</strong> (unchanged — the cost method does not adjust for the investee's post-acquisition performance)<br>
            <strong>Dividend received</strong> = recognised as INCOME in the parent's SEPARATE SOPL, since it derives from post-acquisition profits
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="entry"><span class="dr">Dr</span><div class="etxt">Cash $300,000<div class="badges"><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></div></div></div>
        <div class="entry"><span class="cr">Cr</span><div class="etxt">Dividend income $300,000<div class="badges"><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Dividend Recognition & the Impairment Indicator Test</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">When a dividend signals a possible impairment — worked example</div>
        <div class="principle">An investor recognises a dividend from a subsidiary, joint venture, or associate in profit or loss in its separate financial statements when its right to receive the dividend is established. HOWEVER, when the dividend received/receivable EXCEEDS the total comprehensive income of the subsidiary/JV/associate in the period the dividend is declared, this is an INDICATOR that the investment may be impaired, requiring an IAS 36 impairment assessment.</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Dividend exceeding total comprehensive income</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            A subsidiary declares a dividend of $800,000 to its parent. The subsidiary's total comprehensive income for the SAME period was only $500,000 (the dividend was partly funded from prior years' retained profits or a capital distribution).
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            The $800,000 dividend EXCEEDS the $500,000 total comprehensive income — this is a specific INDICATOR of impairment under IAS 36, requiring the parent to test the carrying amount of its investment in the subsidiary for impairment, since it may signal the subsidiary is depleting its net assets to fund the payout.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">This "dividend exceeds TCI" trigger is a specific, exam-favourite technical rule — it doesn't automatically mean impairment EXISTS, only that an impairment ASSESSMENT is required.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Group Reorganisations — The Cost of a New Parent's Investment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Establishing a new parent above an existing group</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">When a NEW parent is established above an existing group (e.g. for reorganisation purposes), and the new parent obtains control of the original parent by issuing equity instruments in exchange for existing equity instruments, with ownership interests before/after being identical, and the reorganisation has no substance — the new parent measures the cost of its investment in the original parent, in its OWN separate financial statements, at the CARRYING AMOUNT of its share of the equity items shown in the original parent's separate financial statements at the date of reorganisation, NOT at fair value.</div>
        <div class="note">This is a narrow but specific relief that avoids artificially inflating the carrying amount of an investment purely as a result of an internal restructuring with no genuine economic substance.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Changes in Measurement Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Treated as a change in accounting policy under IAS 8</div>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">If an entity changes its measurement approach FROM the cost method TO EITHER the fair value method or the equity method (or vice versa), this is applied as a change in accounting policy in accordance with IAS 8 — meaning RETROSPECTIVE application, restating comparative periods as if the new method had always been used.</div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-bottom:0;"> This links IAS 27 directly to IAS 8's general policy-change machinery — one of the clearer, more testable cross-references in this standard.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Separate vs Consolidated vs Individual — Terminology Clarified</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Three distinct terms that are frequently confused</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Term</span><span>Meaning</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Consolidated financial statements</span><span>The GROUP's statements — parent and subsidiaries presented as a single economic entity (IFRS 10)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Separate financial statements</span><span>The PARENT's OWN statements, presented ALONGSIDE consolidated statements, where investments are measured per IAS 27 (cost, IFRS 9, or equity method)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Individual financial statements</span><span>The financial statements of an entity that has NO subsidiaries, associates, or joint ventures at all — simply its own ordinary financial statements, with no IAS 27 election to make</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Only a parent (or an investor with joint control/significant influence) genuinely prepares "separate" financial statements in the technical IAS 27 sense — the term is a specific label, not a generic synonym for "unconsolidated."</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required disclosures</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">GENERAL DISCLOSURES</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>The fact that the statements are separate financial statements, and the reason they are prepared (if not required by law)</span>
              <span>A list of significant investments in subsidiaries, joint ventures, and associates, including name, principal place of business, proportion of ownership interest, and proportion of voting rights (if different)</span>
              <span>A description of the method used to account for these investments</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">CROSS-REFERENCE DISCLOSURE</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>Identification of the consolidated financial statements (or those prepared applying IAS 28's equity method) to which the separate financial statements relate</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments & Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Which of the three permitted measurement methods to elect for each category of investment, and whether to apply different methods across categories</span>
              <span>Whether a group reorganisation genuinely lacks economic substance, qualifying for the carrying-amount relief in Section 6</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Whether an impairment indicator (e.g. dividend exceeding total comprehensive income) genuinely signals impairment requiring an IAS 36 write-down</span>
              <span>Fair value of investments where the FVTPL/FVOCI option under IFRS 9 is elected</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;"> Since IAS 27 applies only where an entity ALSO prepares consolidated (or IAS 28 equity-method) statements, the standalone "separate" figures should never be read by users as a complete picture of the group's financial position — they specifically represent the parent LEGAL ENTITY in isolation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRS vs US GAAP — Key Differences</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — IAS 27 vs US GAAP</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 27)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Concept of "separate financial statements"</span><span>Explicitly defined and governed by a dedicated standard</span><span>No direct equivalent standalone standard — "parent-only" financial statements are less commonly presented and not separately codified in the same way</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Permitted measurement methods</span><span>Cost, IFRS 9 fair value, or equity method — three explicit options</span><span>Where parent-only statements ARE prepared (e.g. for regulatory purposes), the equity method is typically required rather than offered as a choice among three methods</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Consolidated statements as the primary/required statements</span><span>Required alongside separate statements when control exists (IFRS 10)</span><span>Consolidated statements are similarly the primary required statements under US GAAP consolidation guidance (ASC 810)</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IAS 27's very existence as a standalone standard reflects a distinctly IFRS practice — many jurisdictions applying IFRS require or permit parent-only separate financial statements alongside consolidated ones (often for statutory, dividend-capacity, or tax purposes), a practice less codified under US GAAP.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping — Where Every IAS 27 Event Lands</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table (in the parent's separate financial statements)</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Initial investment in a subsidiary/JV/associate</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Recognised per the elected method (cost, IFRS 9, or equity method)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Dividend received (cost or FVTPL method)</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Recognised as income when the right to receive is established</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Dividend received exceeding investee's TCI</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> + trigger IAS 36 test</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Fair value movement (IFRS 9 FVTPL option)</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Recognised through profit or loss each period</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Fair value movement (IFRS 9 FVOCI election)</span><span><span class="badge bo">OCI</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Never recycled to SOPL, per the irrevocable FVOCI election rules</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Equity method share of profit/loss (equity method option)</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Investment carrying amount adjusted for the investor's share, per IAS 28 mechanics</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Impairment of the investment</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Per IAS 36, applied to the SEPARATE financial statement carrying amount</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Change in measurement method</span><span><span class="badge be">Equity</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Retrospective restatement per IAS 8</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Group reorganisation — new parent's cost of investment</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Measured at carrying amount of the original parent's equity, not fair value, where no substance exists</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>List of significant investments, methods used, cross-reference to consolidated statements</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Mandatory disclosure</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">How IAS 27 connects across the IFRS body of literature</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 10</span><span>Governs consolidated financial statements themselves; IAS 27's separate statements are ALWAYS presented alongside, never instead of, IFRS 10 consolidation when control exists</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 28</span><span>Supplies the equity method mechanics used both in consolidated statements AND as one of the three permitted options in a parent's own separate statements</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 9</span><span>Supplies the fair value measurement option (FVTPL or irrevocable FVOCI) available for investments in a parent's separate financial statements</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 36</span><span>Governs impairment testing of investments carried at cost or under the equity method in separate financial statements, triggered by indicators such as dividends exceeding total comprehensive income</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 8</span><span>Governs the retrospective treatment required when an entity changes its measurement method for investments between the three IAS 27 options</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 3</span><span>Governs the acquisition accounting used to determine the ORIGINAL cost of an investment in a subsidiary recognised in the parent's separate financial statements</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 24</span><span>Subsidiaries, joint ventures, and associates accounted for under IAS 27 are related parties, requiring disclosure of transactions and balances between them and the reporting entity</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="three-col">
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Subsidiary</div><div class="glossary-def">An entity controlled by another entity (the parent), per the control definition in IFRS 10.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Associate</div><div class="glossary-def">An entity over which the investor has significant influence, per IAS 28.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Joint venture</div><div class="glossary-def">A joint arrangement whereby the parties with joint control have rights to the net assets of the arrangement, per IFRS 11.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Equity method</div><div class="glossary-def">A method of accounting whereby the investment is initially recognised at cost and adjusted thereafter for the investor's post-acquisition share of the investee's profit/loss and OCI.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Group</div><div class="glossary-def">A parent and all its subsidiaries, per IFRS 10 — the reporting scope of consolidated financial statements, distinct from the parent-only scope of separate financial statements.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Total comprehensive income</div><div class="glossary-def">Profit or loss plus other comprehensive income for a period — the benchmark used in the dividend-exceeds-TCI impairment indicator test.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="legend">
